  "query_preview": "..\nWater vapor imagery shows a trough along the California coast\nwith a ridge over the central plains. Today will be another warm\nand dry day. Temperatures will be running slightly above normal\nby 1-4 degrees this afternoon. A small pressure gradient has \nformed over the area, bringing southeasterly winds across the\narea. This is due to the approaching lower pressure...the \nCalifornia trough. It can be breezy in the Gila River Valley due\nto the set up. \n\nModel guidance are in a good agreement of the trough moving \nthrough Desert Southwest starting tomorrow morning. Guidance \nalso has been leaning to a dryer solution over the last several \nruns with the best chances for a tenth of an inch by Ajo, north \nof Tucson, and in the White Mountains. The dry front will bring \nabout south to southwesterly breezes across the region. Winds \nspeeds will be 15-20 mph and gusts up to 30 mph, strongest gusts\ncan be expected in Cochise County. \n\nThe upper level pattern will become zonal after the trough exits\nthe region by late Tuesday and early Wednesday. This period \nwill generally be benign and dry until the next weather system \ncoming though on Friday. This second system will nearly follow \nthe previous weather system, but it will be even more dry with \nsome breezes. In this forecast package, wind gusts can be high \nas 25-30 mph and rainfall will be none to very little, favoring \nthe White Mountains area. \n\nTemperatures will follow the roller coaster of below normal to\nslight above normal for the week due to the passing weather \nsystems. Long range ensemble models show another period of\ntroughiness late weekend to early next week, potentially \nleading to a chance for rain showers and breezes.",
